1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,374
Welcome to ShipShape, your autonomous QA crew for Claude Code.

2
00:00:05,704 --> 00:00:12,568
In the next ten minutes we go from zero to an app that tests itself, with real output from a real run.

3
00:00:13,698 --> 00:00:20,466
You give it a goal in plain words, like: signup works. ShipShape plans the checks and runs them.

4
00:00:20,796 --> 00:00:27,440
It reviews the code and its security first, then runs every test twice, records it on video,

5
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:28,716
and narrates it.

6
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:31,820
A load test and a live regression follow,

7
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:37,014
and a refuter tries to prove every failure wrong before anyone files a bug.

8
00:00:38,144 --> 00:00:45,274
It is also careful. It only touches allow-listed local or staging hosts, uses test data, never pays,

9
00:00:45,274 --> 00:00:48,032
and sends nothing until you say send.

10
00:00:48,362 --> 00:00:53,618
Everything in this video runs against a small local demo shop with fake data.

11
00:00:54,748 --> 00:01:01,276
Installing takes two commands. First add the ShipShape marketplace, then install the plugin from it.

12
00:01:01,606 --> 00:01:06,065
Inside Claude Code you can type the same thing as slash plugin commands.

13
00:01:06,065 --> 00:01:09,406
Here the plugin list shows it installed and enabled.

14
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:10,914
On a fresh machine,

15
00:01:10,914 --> 00:01:16,984
the install script in the bin folder sets up Python packages and the Playwright browser for you.

16
00:01:18,115 --> 00:01:26,212
Next, run the doctor. It checks Python, Node, ffmpeg, the Playwright browser, the pinned browser tools,

17
00:01:26,212 --> 00:01:28,219
and the narration voice.

18
00:01:28,549 --> 00:01:34,349
Optional extras, like desktop control or a heavier load engine, are marked optional,

19
00:01:34,349 --> 00:01:35,989
with the fix for each.

20
00:01:36,319 --> 00:01:40,831
Channel secrets are only checked by name. Their values are never printed.

21
00:01:41,961 --> 00:01:48,201
Now the main event. In Claude Code, type /qa-goal, followed by what should work. Here:

22
00:01:48,201 --> 00:01:51,297
signup to welcome works on the demo shop.

23
00:01:51,627 --> 00:01:57,795
The QA lead agent drives seven steps, and hands the judgement calls to specialist agents.

24
00:01:58,927 --> 00:02:04,335
This is the example app that ships with the kit: a tiny sign-up page on localhost.

25
00:02:04,335 --> 00:02:06,103
Everything on it is fake.

26
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:12,640
Step one reads earlier runs, so it knows the baseline and any flaky cases.

27
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:17,217
Step two opens a ledger, which makes the whole run resumable.

28
00:02:17,547 --> 00:02:22,609
Step three is the review. The code reviewer reads the diff before any test runs,

29
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:28,107
and the security pass sweeps for secrets and checks headers on the allow-listed host.

30
00:02:28,437 --> 00:02:32,949
Security findings stay private. Only the count leaves this folder.

31
00:02:34,079 --> 00:02:37,748
Each role is a plain agent file you can read and change:

32
00:02:37,748 --> 00:02:43,175
the code reviewer, the security reviewer, the automation tester, and the refuter.

33
00:02:43,505 --> 00:02:49,337
A workflow file runs them in order, with the review and the security pass side by side.

34
00:02:50,479 --> 00:02:55,179
Step four runs every test plan twice, as two independent passes.

35
00:02:55,179 --> 00:03:01,615
A result only counts if both passes agree. If they disagree, the case is marked flaky.

36
00:03:01,945 --> 00:03:05,239
Here, four checks pass, and one is blocked on purpose:

37
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:10,177
the never-pay rule stopped the run at the Pay now button and asked for a human.

38
00:03:11,313 --> 00:03:16,809
Every pass is recorded in a real browser. This is pass one of the signup plan.

39
00:03:17,139 --> 00:03:20,701
First it submits the form empty and expects a clear error.

40
00:03:20,701 --> 00:03:26,043
Then it fills in a test user with an example address and expects the welcome message.

41
00:03:26,373 --> 00:03:33,069
Screenshots, a Playwright trace, console errors and failed network calls are all saved as evidence.

42
00:03:34,213 --> 00:03:36,338
Step five is the load test.

43
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:44,725
The smoke profile sent forty four requests, with a p95 of about sixty seven milliseconds and zero errors.

44
00:03:45,055 --> 00:03:47,755
Ramp, soak and spike profiles are there too,

45
00:03:47,755 --> 00:03:52,663
and anything that looks like production is refused unless the owner allows it.

46
00:03:53,793 --> 00:04:00,695
The live regression recorder replays your regression list as a real user, read-only on staging or live.

47
00:04:00,695 --> 00:04:04,233
Here is the narrated video the kit produced itself.

48
00:04:04,513 --> 00:04:09,923
We open the local demo shop's sign-up page. Everything here is fake test data.

49
00:04:10,106 --> 00:04:14,076
First, a negative case: we submit the form empty.

50
00:04:14,712 --> 00:04:18,250
The form refuses an empty name and says why.

51
00:04:18,908 --> 00:04:22,902
Now we fill in a test user with a fake example address.

52
00:04:23,135 --> 00:04:27,513
The account is created and the welcome message greets the test user.

53
00:04:35,663 --> 00:04:41,163
Now a failure. A second plan expects a returning user to see: welcome back.

54
00:04:41,163 --> 00:04:46,271
Both passes fail, so the loop pauses and asks for verify and refute.

55
00:04:47,401 --> 00:04:50,502
The refuter is the second agent of a two-agent bar.

56
00:04:50,502 --> 00:04:54,697
It works on its own path and tries hard to prove the failure wrong.

57
00:04:55,027 --> 00:05:00,997
Its verdict: refuted. Neither the app nor the original plan ever promised welcome back.

58
00:05:00,997 --> 00:05:04,411
The test expectation was wrong, not the product.

59
00:05:04,741 --> 00:05:08,325
So no bug is filed, and the ledger shows zero open bugs.

60
00:05:08,325 --> 00:05:12,349
That is how ShipShape keeps false alarms out of your tracker.

61
00:05:13,479 --> 00:05:21,567
Step six writes one self-contained HTML report. It opens offline, so you can attach it anywhere.

62
00:05:21,897 --> 00:05:27,115
At the top, the verdict and the counts: four pass, zero fail, one blocked.

63
00:05:27,115 --> 00:05:32,313
Then every case with its evidence, and the videos you can play in place.

64
00:05:32,643 --> 00:05:33,581
Further down:

65
00:05:33,581 --> 00:05:40,294
console and network checks, the load chart, bugs that survived the refuter, open human gates,

66
00:05:40,294 --> 00:05:42,291
and the trend across runs.

67
00:05:42,621 --> 00:05:47,973
There is also a spoken summary, so a non-technical owner can just listen to the result.

68
00:05:49,112 --> 00:05:56,768
Step seven is delivery to Telegram, WhatsApp, Slack, email or a webhook, whatever your config lists.

69
00:05:57,098 --> 00:06:01,638
First a privacy scan of everything that would leave: zero findings.

70
00:06:01,638 --> 00:06:07,178
Then a dry run that prints exactly what would be sent, with the tokens left out.

71
00:06:07,508 --> 00:06:13,868
Nothing leaves the machine until the owner says send, and a channel that is not in the config is refused.

72
00:06:15,012 --> 00:06:21,252
The guard rails are not just advice. The plugin ships hooks that Claude Code runs before risky tools,

73
00:06:21,252 --> 00:06:24,276
and most of them are written in plain language.

74
00:06:24,606 --> 00:06:30,606
For example: block a git push that force-pushes, or pushes to main or production.

75
00:06:31,745 --> 00:06:37,158
Here we asked Claude, with the plugin loaded, to force-push to main in a throwaway repo.

76
00:06:37,158 --> 00:06:40,169
The hook blocked it before git was even called.

77
00:06:40,499 --> 00:06:43,721
A second, deterministic hook scans every commit.

78
00:06:43,721 --> 00:06:50,634
A fake bot token was staged by mistake, so the commit is blocked, and only the kind and line are shown,

79
00:06:50,634 --> 00:06:51,827
never the value.

80
00:06:52,957 --> 00:06:58,545
Some flows live outside the browser: native apps, file pickers, OS dialogs.

81
00:06:58,545 --> 00:07:03,061
ShipShape can drive the desktop too, but only behind gates.

82
00:07:03,391 --> 00:07:08,174
With desktop control off in the config, the first step is refused.

83
00:07:08,174 --> 00:07:14,743
Turned on, only allow-listed apps run, and the delete step stops and asks the user first.

84
00:07:15,073 --> 00:07:20,628
Banking, payment and password apps are always refused, and every action is logged.

85
00:07:20,628 --> 00:07:24,745
These are dry runs, so nothing on this desktop was touched.

86
00:07:25,891 --> 00:07:32,777
The kit is not only QA. /timesheet measures real working hours from Claude Code session logs,

87
00:07:32,777 --> 00:07:36,451
merging sub-agents so parallel work counts once.

88
00:07:36,781 --> 00:07:43,803
Idle gaps are cut, blocks are mapped to tasks, and you get an Excel sheet plus Odoo import rows.

89
00:07:43,803 --> 00:07:46,045
This run uses synthetic logs.

90
00:07:46,375 --> 00:07:51,559
It never writes to Odoo by itself. A human reviews the file first.

91
00:07:52,691 --> 00:07:59,795
/odoo-check runs pre-deploy checks on an Odoo module. This fake loyalty module has four problems.

92
00:08:00,125 --> 00:08:06,968
A cron outside a no-update block, no neutralize script, a raw commit inside a model, and no tests.

93
00:08:06,968 --> 00:08:10,301
Each one is a classic way to break production.

94
00:08:10,631 --> 00:08:15,071
The Odoo expert agent then explains each fix before the deploy.

95
00:08:16,201 --> 00:08:21,361
When you get stuck, run the doctor first. It prints the fix for anything missing.

96
00:08:21,691 --> 00:08:28,051
The README and the docs folder cover every command, and you can simply ask Claude how to use any of them.

97
00:08:28,381 --> 00:08:34,861
For questions or bugs, open an issue on the repository. Thanks for watching, and happy shipping.

